# How omdrop-owl runs AWDL on each Wi-Fi driver. One line per kernel driver,
# named as in /sys/class/net/<iface>/device/driver. A card whose driver isn't
# listed falls back to the "*" line, and only runs with /etc/omdrop/allow-untested.
# /etc/omdrop/owl-profiles, in the same format, is read before this file: try
# settings for your card there. To add your card here, see README.md, "Adding
# your card".
#
#   status     tested: works out of the box; untested: needs allow-untested
#   mode       shared: AWDL shares the AP's channel and Wi-Fi stays connected
#              exclusive: Wi-Fi is disconnected for the window, for cards that
#              can't inject, or hear AWDL, while associated (README)
#              active-monitor: exclusive, but with an active monitor in place of
#              the ad-hoc interface, for cards with no ad-hoc mode. Channel 6
#              only, and needs mon_mac station
#   mon_mac    station: the monitor interface carries the station's MAC, and OWL
#              reads it from there; own: it keeps a MAC of its own and OWL is
#              given the station's (owl -m)
#   reconnect  yes: force a reconnect after deleting the monitor interface, for
#              drivers whose station stops receiving after that
#   mt76_pm    yes: turn mt76 runtime power management off for the window
#   notes      the rest of the line: the hardware, and what was observed
#
# driver   status    mode    mon_mac  reconnect  mt76_pm  notes
mt7925e    tested    shared  station  yes        yes      MediaTek MT7925 (Framework 13). Injects while associated, sending from the station's MAC; the station ACKs the phone's unicast. Without a reconnect, the station stops receiving 3-165 s after a monitor interface is deleted.
mt7921e    untested  active-monitor  station  no  yes      MediaTek MT7921 (14c3:7961, kernel 7.2.9) and MT7922 (14c3:0616, kernels 6.18 and 7.2.5). No ad-hoc mode. Both ways with an iPhone on channel 6, but not reliably: the driver sends injected unicast at 1 Mbit/s CCK whatever rate OWL asks for, so a full frame fills most of the one slot in sixteen the phone spends on channel 6, and on 5 GHz none of it reaches the air (README, "Known issues"). Runtime PM is off for the window, as in both successful tests of the driver rate fix (#14); with it on, one of two transfers from an iPhone stalled.
mt76x2u    tested    active-monitor  station  no  no       MediaTek MT7612U USB (0e8d:7612), kernel 7.2.9. Both ways with an iPhone; sending uses BLE wake-up.
iwlwifi    tested    exclusive  station  no       no       Intel AX200 (kernel 6.19.8), AX201 (kernels 6.8 and 7.2.9), AX211 (kernels 7.0 and 7.2.3), BE on Panther Lake (kernel 7.2) and 9560 (kernel 7.2.8). Associated, the firmware passes up no AWDL frames. Starts no ad-hoc cell on 5 GHz, so runs on channel 6: a 2.4 MB photo from an iPhone in 17 s, to it in 10.5 s. Some AX211s refuse an ad-hoc vif beside the station, so the station becomes the cell.
rtw88_8822be  tested  exclusive  station  no     no       Realtek RTL8822BE (kernel 7.2.9). Shared mode is untried. The ad-hoc vif sits beside the station. Starts no ad-hoc cell on 5 GHz (EINVAL on 44 and 149, neither "No IR"), so runs on channel 6: a 6.4 MB photo from an iPhone in 110 s. The kernel logs "failed to get tx report from firmware" through the window. The station is back about 6 s after teardown and stayed up for the five minutes watched, without a forced reconnect.
rtw89_8852ae  untested  shared  station  yes     no       Realtek RTL8852AE (ThinkPad P14s Gen 2a, kernel 7.0). Both directions on channel 6, in separate runs. After teardown, 280/300 pings with an 8 s gap despite the reconnect, on an AP that was already dropping pings beforehand. Whether reconnect is needed is untested.
*          untested  shared  station  yes        no       Any other card. Needs monitor-mode injection while associated, and an AP on channel 6, 44 or 149.
